    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10b-2010-p14.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Recall that the sum of the first \\(n\\) integers is \\(\\dfrac{n(n + 1)}{2}.\\)\n\nThen, we have that \\[ \\dfrac{\\frac{99 \\cdot 100}{2} + x}{100} = 100x, \\] which simplifies to \\[ 99 \\cdot 50 = (100^2 - 1)x\\]\\[ = 101 \\cdot 99x, \\] by difference of squares. Dividing gives us \\(x = \\dfrac{50}{101}.\\)\n\nThus, B is the correct answer.",
      "hints": [
        "Use the sum \\(1+2+\\cdots+99\\)",
        "Set \\(\\dfrac{99\\cdot50+x}{100}=100x\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
